## Keep what matches

Filter means keep only the items that match a query, then show that shorter list. `filterList(items, q)` should return the matching items joined by commas. Matching here means the item includes the query string, so `"l"` keeps `"lamp"` and `"loop"` and drops `"fuse"`. The original array can stay as it was; you return a new, thinner result.

This bites when you return the whole list every time. The screen still shows `fuse`, so the query did no work. It also bites when you write a loop that rebuilds the list by hand and forget `filter` — the check looks for that word because the habit is the point of this step.

```javascript
function filterList(items, q) {
  return items.filter((item) => item.includes(q)).join(",");
}
```

A common mistake is joining every item, or hardcoding `"lamp,loop"`. Both skip the query. The exercise asks you to filter `["lamp", "fuse", "loop"]` by `"l"` so the printed line is `lamp,loop`, using `filter`.
